[00:22:30] ##############################################################################################################
[00:22:30] # EVERY QUESTION IS CHARGED. Tree questions come from the same combined pool as the global build (top-500 popular items + top-500 answerable concepts); they burn an interview turn and their answers are folded into the recommender exactly like any other answer -- an asked-but-unrated item as the i26 native UNSEEN exposure token, an unanswerable concept as a cv3 refusal token. There is NO free side information in this arm and therefore NO generosity caveat: the total budget is 15 questions on both sides at every K.
[00:22:30] # The MIN-LEAF RULE IS THE ONLY STOPPING RULE -- there is no depth cap. A node is not split if ANY of its three children would hold fewer than 200 validation users; growth otherwise continues until the interview budget itself runs out. Realised depth is a RESULT, not a setting. The 200 is empirical, from the genre-split experiment (2026-08-11): every genre group ABOVE 200 users held a positive gain when its order was carried to the TEST cohort, while every group BELOW it failed to generalise (Horror n=51 test -0.0013 ns; Crime n=79 test -0.0433; Fantasy n=11 test -0.1156). Applied to the CHOSEN splitter, not as a search constraint: if the argmax question shatters the node, the node STOPS -- it does not fall back to a worse question.
[00:22:30] # Concept answer values follow the CERTIFIED-ERA convention that `concept_cv3_ep08` was trained under and that produced the comparator: uncapped signed values, with `cap_negatives` applied over the REVEALED set only, C_NEG read from the signed prereg. This is `build_greedy_env`'s own path and is checked against `signed_answers.cap_negatives` by `contract_selftest` at startup. (`--concept_neg_cap` is an `adaptive_harness` flag; it does not exist on this code path, and introducing one would put this arm on a different ruler from its own comparator.)
[00:22:30] # THE BANKED GLOBAL BUILD IS A POLICY-A ARTIFACT (measured 2026-08-12, this run's sanity gate). `greedy_static_i26c.json` records intercept.full = 0.12987336, which is the POLICY-A m=0 value; today's harness scores under POLICY B (2026-08-05 ruling, candidate_masking_protocol.md) and gives m=0 = 0.15686538 on the same 10,000 validation users. Its stored `gains` and `curve` are therefore on a DIFFERENT RULER and are NOT comparable to anything measured here -- numbers from different information contracts are not ordered. What IS used from that file is the SEQUENCE of questions, which is re-scored IN THIS RUN under policy B for every comparison (the same thing `group_greedy_genre.py` does). Residual caveat, stated rather than buried: the comparator's ORDER was selected under policy A, so it is not exactly 'the same objective under the same protocol'. Closing that would mean rebuilding the static combined order under policy B (~12 h).
[00:22:30] # A leaf's order must carry its ancestors' questions, which were chosen for their PARENTS' cohorts. The global order is therefore NOT inside a leaf's search space and a leaf CAN score below it. A negative leaf gain is a RESULT here, not a harness failure. The one mechanical guarantee is that the root splitter equals the global order's first question, so the K=1 difference is identically zero; that is asserted against the banked global build.
